1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,980
In this video, we'll continue
with our discussion of

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:05,410
processes and talk
more about daemons,

3
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:08,215
which we introduced briefly
in a previous video.

4
00:00:08,215 --> 00:00:11,205
The learning objectives here.

5
00:00:11,205 --> 00:00:12,370
We're going to talk about

6
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,425
process user groups
and permissions,

7
00:00:14,425 --> 00:00:16,060
we're going to talk
about the PS utility,

8
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:18,780
which you can use to
examine process status,

9
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:22,320
and we're going to talk about
introduction to daemons.

10
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:26,800
There's multiple user and

11
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,855
group IDs that are
associated with a process.

12
00:00:28,855 --> 00:00:30,880
You can track things
like who originally ran

13
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,265
the process or who originally
started the process,

14
00:00:33,265 --> 00:00:34,960
so that would be referred

15
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,920
to as the real
user and group ID.

16
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:38,605
Then you can track also

17
00:00:38,605 --> 00:00:40,260
who the process is
currently running as,

18
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:42,435
so that's the effective user ID,

19
00:00:42,435 --> 00:00:44,840
which is probably what
you're most interested in,

20
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:46,565
because you want to
know what permissions

21
00:00:46,565 --> 00:00:49,225
the process would have.

22
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:51,970
The effective user
ID for a process.

23
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:53,600
The reason we track
them separately is

24
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,670
because the effective
user ID can be changed.

25
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,160
There's SetUID or SetGID

26
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,050
that can change the
effective user ID

27
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:01,465
for the process that's run.

28
00:01:01,465 --> 00:01:05,270
There are ways to
get the real or

29
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:06,980
the effective user and group

30
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:10,020
ID through these project calls.

31
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,720
You might think, if
I have the option to

32
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:19,145
run or change to use
different effective user IDs,

33
00:01:19,145 --> 00:01:22,285
what user and group should
I be using for my process?

34
00:01:22,285 --> 00:01:24,635
There's a couple of
different options you have.

35
00:01:24,635 --> 00:01:26,770
One is you could use
the root account.

36
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:27,890
That would ensure that you

37
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:29,120
have access to
whatever you need.

38
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:30,890
Basically, the root account
is going to be able

39
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:33,100
to view pretty much
everything in the system,

40
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,365
or you could use some
specific other account.

41
00:01:36,365 --> 00:01:39,860
When you're trying to decide
which you should use,

42
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:41,630
there's the doctrine of

43
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:43,070
least-privileged rights that you

44
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:44,900
can use to help
make this decision.

45
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:47,000
The doctrine of
least-privileged rights

46
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,470
says that a process should

47
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:49,910
ideally execute with

48
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:52,435
the minimum level
of rights possible.

49
00:01:52,435 --> 00:01:54,650
Why would we want to execute

50
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:56,645
with a minimum level
of rights possible?

51
00:01:56,645 --> 00:01:58,825
Isn't that going to make
our lives more difficult?

52
00:01:58,825 --> 00:02:01,280
In some ways it
will because it's

53
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:02,495
easier to just run as root

54
00:02:02,495 --> 00:02:04,100
and be able to access anything.

55
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,280
But the reason you want to
use the minimum level of

56
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:08,660
rights is because
you're going to be

57
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:10,555
less susceptible to exploit.

58
00:02:10,555 --> 00:02:12,200
In other words, if
someone's able to

59
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,155
make your process
do the wrong thing,

60
00:02:14,155 --> 00:02:16,040
it's less likely that

61
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:17,180
they're going to be
able to do something

62
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,710
malicious if you aren't

63
00:02:18,710 --> 00:02:20,875
running with right
privileges than if you are.

64
00:02:20,875 --> 00:02:22,415
The second reason is that you're

65
00:02:22,415 --> 00:02:24,170
less susceptible to
your own errors.

66
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:27,100
We've given this
example before of rm-rf

67
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:30,665
of sum mistypedd var and slash.

68
00:02:30,665 --> 00:02:31,880
If you run that as a root,

69
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:33,350
it's going to start
deleting the contents

70
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:34,645
out of your real file system,

71
00:02:34,645 --> 00:02:38,230
because mistypedd var is
going to evaluate to nothing.

72
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:40,580
This is just one example of

73
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,350
a case where you can
get yourself into

74
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:44,030
trouble if you give

75
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:46,220
yourself more provisions than
you need in your process.

76
00:02:46,220 --> 00:02:47,540
You always want to
try to execute with

77
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,430
a minimum level of rights
that are possible.

78
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:54,290
The Chapter 5 talks a

79
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:56,615
little bit about process
groups and sessions.

80
00:02:56,615 --> 00:02:59,330
These are just ways to
group process together.

81
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:01,250
The process group
is a collection of

82
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:02,930
processes and then a session

83
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:04,735
is a collection of
process groups.

84
00:03:04,735 --> 00:03:07,475
A session is associated with
a controlling terminal.

85
00:03:07,475 --> 00:03:09,290
What do we mean by a
controlling terminal?

86
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:11,510
It means the terminal
that you're using for

87
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:14,320
login in a text-based
operation, for instance.

88
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,055
Where you enter your
login information,

89
00:03:16,055 --> 00:03:17,845
where you enter your
shell commands.

90
00:03:17,845 --> 00:03:21,140
For historical reasons,
this is known as a TTY,

91
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:23,075
which stands for teletypewriter.

92
00:03:23,075 --> 00:03:25,930
You can see that that's
a historical term.

93
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:29,490
But a TTY is just a
device terminal I/O.

94
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:31,290
Think of keyboard interaction,

95
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:32,745
login, command I/O.

96
00:03:32,745 --> 00:03:35,000
That's really what a
session is all about.

97
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,310
It's created for the login
shell on a specific TTY.

98
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:39,900
You might have different TTYs,

99
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:42,515
you might have a serial
port you can login over,

100
00:03:42,515 --> 00:03:44,870
and you might have virtual
terminals that you can

101
00:03:44,870 --> 00:03:47,860
login over in a terminal
window for instance.

102
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:50,800
They'll be sessions associate
with each of those.

103
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,820
There's always one single

104
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:55,175
foreground process
group within a session.

105
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:57,365
This is the one that
if you hit Control C,

106
00:03:57,365 --> 00:03:59,000
you're going to interrupt
whatever's running in

107
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,640
that foreground process group

108
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:03,790
for the session you
sent the Control C to.

109
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:06,905
Then there's also zero or more
background process group.

110
00:04:06,905 --> 00:04:10,760
If you want to start something
in the background shell,

111
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,120
you can use the ampersand
after the shell commands.

112
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:15,655
If you have a long
running command,

113
00:04:15,655 --> 00:04:17,265
you had to type the
name of the command,

114
00:04:17,265 --> 00:04:18,180
and then a space,

115
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,700
and put the ampersand,
that's going

116
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:21,335
to put it in the
background process group,

117
00:04:21,335 --> 00:04:22,670
which means you'll
still be able to

118
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:24,860
use the foreground for

119
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,740
shell activity or shell

120
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:31,610
commands while the process is
running in the background.

121
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:34,670
How do I see what
processes are running,

122
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:36,425
including those processes from,

123
00:04:36,425 --> 00:04:39,020
let's say, different sessions
or from the background?

124
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:42,560
Ps is what we can use to show
status about the process.

125
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:44,000
This is the utility
that we can use

126
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,775
on the command line
for this purpose.

127
00:04:45,775 --> 00:04:48,800
There are lots of different
ways to use this.

128
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:52,130
For whatever reason, I've
always used this ps axu,

129
00:04:52,130 --> 00:04:54,035
probably just a force of habit,

130
00:04:54,035 --> 00:04:56,930
but there's a lot of
information you can collect

131
00:04:56,930 --> 00:05:00,425
about this relative to
current running processes.

132
00:05:00,425 --> 00:05:02,090
Depending on how you invoke it,

133
00:05:02,090 --> 00:05:05,165
you get something like
this in terms of output.

134
00:05:05,165 --> 00:05:07,310
This will show you
at a glance which

135
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:10,130
user account is
running which process.

136
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:12,950
Here I had an ecen5013 user.

137
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:16,940
That's where you can see
there's some activity going

138
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:21,380
on here related to a
bash session and build.

139
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:23,240
You could see all the
different processes

140
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,020
that are kicked off from

141
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,510
my user account and you could

142
00:05:26,510 --> 00:05:29,915
also see these processes
that are running as root.

143
00:05:29,915 --> 00:05:32,135
Incidentally these
with the brackets,

144
00:05:32,135 --> 00:05:34,175
this is showing kernel threads.

145
00:05:34,175 --> 00:05:36,695
Those will always
be running as root.

146
00:05:36,695 --> 00:05:40,550
Then one other thing that you
might now think of but it's

147
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:42,200
also going to show
you the command that

148
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,165
you execute here
on the terminal.

149
00:05:44,165 --> 00:05:45,815
It's going to show us ps, aux,

150
00:05:45,815 --> 00:05:49,640
and less being the two
commands that we entered here.

151
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:52,070
When I pipe to less,
what this is just doing

152
00:05:52,070 --> 00:05:53,900
is rather than rolling

153
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:55,760
off the screen if I
have a lot of output,

154
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:58,710
it's going to do
screen by screen.

155
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:03,620
Daemons, originally
the intention

156
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:05,315
was to pronounce dee-men.

157
00:06:05,315 --> 00:06:07,385
It's also commonly
pronounced day-men,

158
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:08,855
you can hear that that's

159
00:06:08,855 --> 00:06:10,340
the way I've been
pronouncing it.

160
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:12,395
That's my force of habit there.

161
00:06:12,395 --> 00:06:14,540
A daemon is just a
process that runs in

162
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:15,710
the background and does not

163
00:06:15,710 --> 00:06:17,300
connect to your
controlling terminal.

164
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:20,990
It's typically started at
boot time and it's run as

165
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:23,030
either root or a special
user depending on

166
00:06:23,030 --> 00:06:25,460
what permissions you
need for your daemon.

167
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:27,740
Often this ends with
a d in the name,

168
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:30,230
so sshd or crond are

169
00:06:30,230 --> 00:06:34,205
two examples of popular
daemon names for ssh or cron.

170
00:06:34,205 --> 00:06:38,090
Often a daemon will run as a
child of init just because

171
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:39,890
the init process is
going to be the one that

172
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:43,160
starts these, each
one of the deamons.

173
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,400
The book goes through
how you can create

174
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:49,460
a daemon using the
functions that we talked

175
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:52,400
about in the previous video

176
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:55,325
regarding starting
a new process.

177
00:06:55,325 --> 00:06:58,130
Fork will create that new
child process that's going

178
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:00,590
to become the daemon
and then exit

179
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:02,315
in the parent is going to allow

180
00:07:02,315 --> 00:07:06,185
the grandparent in that
case init to continue.

181
00:07:06,185 --> 00:07:08,390
You want to kick off the daemon

182
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:10,670
and then continue running.

183
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:14,405
Rather than wait for
the daemon to complete,

184
00:07:14,405 --> 00:07:15,845
you'll want to exit so that

185
00:07:15,845 --> 00:07:18,920
the init process can keep
starting new daemons.

186
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:20,990
Then setsid is going

187
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:22,970
to create a new session
and that's going to

188
00:07:22,970 --> 00:07:23,630
ensure that there's

189
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:26,690
no controlling terminal
because again,

190
00:07:26,690 --> 00:07:28,100
we don't really
want to associate

191
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:29,690
a daemon with
controlling terminal.

192
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:31,130
But it's possible that it

193
00:07:31,130 --> 00:07:32,915
might be depending
on how it started.

194
00:07:32,915 --> 00:07:37,430
Chdir/ is going to change
the working directory.

195
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:40,610
The main reason for doing that
would be if you start from

196
00:07:40,610 --> 00:07:42,980
a directory and then

197
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:44,720
later wanted to delete
that directory,

198
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,075
you might have an error saying
the directory is in use.

199
00:07:47,075 --> 00:07:49,235
That could be confusing.

200
00:07:49,235 --> 00:07:52,145
Then close all the
open file descriptors

201
00:07:52,145 --> 00:07:53,930
and then redirect standard in,

202
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:56,465
standard out, and standard
error to dev/null.

203
00:07:56,465 --> 00:07:59,960
Why would you want to
redirect to dev/null?

204
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:02,030
If you didn't
redirect to dev/null,

205
00:08:02,030 --> 00:08:03,515
it will end up going
to the terminal.

206
00:08:03,515 --> 00:08:05,900
That can also be a confusing
thing if you've got

207
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:07,610
this thing in the
background that

208
00:08:07,610 --> 00:08:10,490
suddenly starts spitting
content out to the terminal.

209
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:13,740
That's the reason for
redirecting nodes.

210
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:19,745
Why would we not want to just
redirect to a file instead?

211
00:08:19,745 --> 00:08:21,440
Well again, you can end up

212
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:23,810
setting up a relationship
there between

213
00:08:23,810 --> 00:08:25,970
the file system and the
daemon that could be hard

214
00:08:25,970 --> 00:08:28,820
to expect or troubleshoot.

215
00:08:28,820 --> 00:08:31,310
What if the files that
you're writing to is on

216
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:32,810
a file system that
is trying to be

217
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:34,550
unmounted by the
user, for instance?

218
00:08:34,550 --> 00:08:36,350
Then the user will get errors

219
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:39,360
saying that the file
system is in use.

220
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:43,955
If I'm not going to use
standard in standard out,

221
00:08:43,955 --> 00:08:45,785
what's the best way
that I can give

222
00:08:45,785 --> 00:08:47,705
user status and errors

223
00:08:47,705 --> 00:08:50,360
about status or give

224
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:51,290
feedback about errors that are

225
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:52,520
happening within the daemon.

226
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:54,605
You can use a logging
framework like syslog,

227
00:08:54,605 --> 00:08:57,480
like we talked about in Week 1.

228
00:08:57,490 --> 00:09:00,650
I did want to point out when
we're talking about daemons,

229
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:02,870
that there are different
ways to do daemons.

230
00:09:02,870 --> 00:09:07,250
Some of the newer ways
are related to system d,

231
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:11,585
which is a new way of
starting init processes.

232
00:09:11,585 --> 00:09:14,090
But I think it's
valuable to go through

233
00:09:14,090 --> 00:09:17,150
the init related or the daemon

234
00:09:17,150 --> 00:09:19,610
related content that's
in the book because this

235
00:09:19,610 --> 00:09:20,840
is the classic way to do

236
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:22,550
it and you're definitely
going to see this.

237
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:24,230
But I just wanted
to bring this up so

238
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:25,850
that you know that there
are different schools of

239
00:09:25,850 --> 00:09:27,965
thought about how to
create a daemon and

240
00:09:27,965 --> 00:09:29,480
it's not necessarily that

241
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:31,175
this is the only way
to create a daemon.

242
00:09:31,175 --> 00:09:33,380
There's a couple of links
in here with people saying

243
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:35,390
exactly the opposite
things about

244
00:09:35,390 --> 00:09:37,370
how to start a daemon
and there's good reasons

245
00:09:37,370 --> 00:09:40,220
to do them different ways.

246
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,410
If you want, you could read more

247
00:09:42,410 --> 00:09:44,510
about the different schools

248
00:09:44,510 --> 00:09:46,085
of thought about how
to use a daemon.

249
00:09:46,085 --> 00:09:48,890
But for our purposes
in this class,

250
00:09:48,890 --> 00:09:51,140
we'll follow the book's
example and we'll use

251
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:53,510
the classic methods for

252
00:09:53,510 --> 00:09:56,460
creating a daemon that
are discussed there.